Q: (GNU) C23: How to define an constant array as a constant expression in C but keeping the defintion in a seperate C file? A: <p><code>extern const</code> doesn't make the value a compile-time constant in C, it just tells the compiler that the object is defined somewhere else, so <code>pin_definitions[12]</code> still can't be used where a constant expression is required.</p>
<p>If you only need the number of entries or a few values at compile time, I'd put those values in the header instead of trying to get them from the array:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>// pin_definitions.h
#define PIN_COUNT 127

extern const pin_definition_t pin_definitions[PIN_COUNT];
</code></pre>
<p>Then</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>// pin_definitions.c
const pin_definition_t pin_definitions[PIN_COUNT] = {
    ...
};
</code></pre>
<p>If you actually need to share the data between the <code>.c</code> files without having multiple copies of the table, an X-macro is one option, let's take a look</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>// pin_data.def
X(0, 0x0000000a, 0xea)
X(1, 0x0000000f, 0x12)
</code></pre>
<p>You can include that file when defining the actual array:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#define X(id, reg, mode) { reg, mode },

const pin_definition_t pin_definitions[] = {
    #include &quot;pin_data.def&quot;
};

#undef X
</code></pre>
<p>And you can include the same data file elsewhere with a different definition of <code>X</code> if you need the values in another form.</p>
<p>If all you're trying to avoid is having a <code>static const</code> table duplicated in multiple translation units, I'd also check whether LTO is enough for your case, GCC can often remove/deduplicate identical read-only data when compiling with <code>-flto</code>, so you may not need the X-macro approach at all.</p>

Q: JSON Serializing java.sql.Time, java.sql.Date, and java.sql.Timestamp with minimal allocation A: <p>You can drop both <code>GregorianCalendar</code>s and get true zero-allocation extraction with pure integer math, the same epoch-day algorithm <code>java.time.LocalDate</code> uses internally (<a href="http://howardhinnant.github.io/date_algorithms.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Hinnant's <code>civil_from_days</code></a>):</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>private static void civilFromEpochDay(long epochDay, int[] ymd) {
    long z = epochDay + 719_468L;
    long era = Math.floorDiv(z, 146_097L);
    long doe = z - era * 146_097L;
    long yoe = (doe - doe/1460 + doe/36524 - doe/146096) / 365;
    long y   = yoe + era * 400;
    long doy = doe - (365*yoe + yoe/4 - yoe/100);
    long mp  = (5*doy + 2) / 153;
    ymd[2] = (int) (doy - (153*mp + 2)/5 + 1);          // day
    ymd[1] = (int) (mp + (mp &lt; 10 ? 3 : -9));           // month
    ymd[0] = (int) (y + (ymd[1] &lt;= 2 ? 1 : 0));         // year
}
</code></pre>
<p>Time-of-day is trivial integer division once you have ms-of-day (<code>s = msOfDay/1000, hour = s/3600, min = (s/60)%60, sec = s%60</code>).</p>
<p>Use <code>Math.floorDiv/floorMod</code> (not <code>//%</code>) to get <code>epochDay/millisOfDay</code> from <code>getTime()</code>, so pre-1970 dates still work.</p>
<p>For <code>Time</code>'s local-zone offset: <code>TimeZone.getDefault()</code> clones on every call, cache it once as a field, same as your calendars:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>private final TimeZone localZone = TimeZone.getDefault(); // fetch ONCE
</code></pre>
<p><code>localZone.getOffset(millis)</code> itself returns a primitive <code>int</code> with no allocation.
Add that offset to the raw millis before computing millis-of-day for <code>Time</code>, and you've eliminated the <code>Calendar</code>s entirely.</p>

Q: ImageData not changing Canvas A: <p>Function <code>createImageData()</code> creates data only with zeros<br />
and this means <code>0</code> also for <code>alpha</code> channel - so all pixels are transparent.</p>
<p>You have to change transparency for every pixel:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>imgData.data[i + 3] = 255;  // alpha: fully visible
</code></pre>
<p>And this solves your problem.</p>
<hr />
<p>Full working code:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
    &lt;canvas id=&quot;canvas&quot; width=&quot;100&quot; height=&quot;100&quot; style=&quot;border:1px solid #000&quot;&gt;&lt;/canvas&gt;
    &lt;br&gt;
    &lt;button onclick=&quot;testFunction()&quot;&gt; Test &lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;

&lt;script type=&quot;text/javascript&quot;&gt;
function testFunction() {
    var canvas = document.getElementById(&quot;canvas&quot;);
    var ctx = canvas.getContext(&quot;2d&quot;);
    var imgData = ctx.createImageData(100, 100);
    for (let i = 0; i &lt; imgData.data.length; i+=4) {

        imgData.data[i + 3] = 255;  // alpha: fully visible

        if (i%2 == 0) {
            imgData.data[i] = 255;
        } else {
            imgData.data[i] = 0;
            imgData.data[i+1] = 0;
            imgData.data[i+2] = 0;
        }
    }
    ctx.putImageData(imgData,0,0);
}
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>By the way:</p>
<p><code>i += 4</code> gives value <code>0</code>, <code>4</code>, <code>8</code>, <code>12</code>, ... and all of then gives <code>0</code> for <code>i%2</code><br />
and it never executes <code>else</code>.</p>
<p>So you can do the same with</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>    for (let i = 0; i &lt; imgData.data.length; i+=4) {

        imgData.data[i + 3] = 255;  // alpha: fully visible

        imgData.data[i] = 255;  // red
    }
</code></pre>
<p>If you want red-white stripes then it may need <code>i += 8</code> (without <code>else</code>).<br />
(more precisely it skips some pixels and they are still transparent and they show background color)</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>
    for (let i = 0; i &lt; imgData.data.length; i+=8) {  // &lt;--- i += 8

        imgData.data[i + 3] = 255;  // alpha: fully visible

        imgData.data[i] = 255;  // red
    }
</code></pre>
<p>If you want to create stripes with other color (instead of <code>white</code>) then it may need to check <code>i%8</code> (and still use <code>i += 4</code> and <code>else</code>)</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>    for (let i = 0; i &lt; imgData.data.length; i+=4) {

        imgData.data[i + 3] = 255;  // alpha: fully visible

        if(i%8 == 0) {
            imgData.data[i] = 255;  // red
        } else {
            imgData.data[i+1] = 255;  // green
        }
    }
</code></pre>
<p>Full working code used for tests:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
    &lt;canvas id=&quot;canvas&quot; width=&quot;100&quot; height=&quot;100&quot; style=&quot;border:1px solid #000&quot;&gt;&lt;/canvas&gt;
    &lt;br&gt;
    &lt;button onclick=&quot;testFunction0()&quot;&gt; Test 0 (Original) &lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;button onclick=&quot;testFunction1()&quot;&gt; Test 1 (RED) &lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;button onclick=&quot;testFunction2()&quot;&gt; Test 2 (stripes RED/BACKGROUND) &lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;button onclick=&quot;testFunction3()&quot;&gt; Test 3 (stripes RED/GREEN) &lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;

&lt;script type=&quot;text/javascript&quot;&gt;
function testFunction0() {   // Original
    var canvas = document.getElementById(&quot;canvas&quot;);
    var ctx = canvas.getContext(&quot;2d&quot;);
    var imgData = ctx.createImageData(100, 100);
    for (let i = 0; i &lt; imgData.data.length; i+=4) {

        imgData.data[i + 3] = 255;  // alpha: fully visible

        if (i%2 == 0) {
            imgData.data[i] = 255;  // red
        } else {
            imgData.data[i] = 0;
            imgData.data[i+1] = 0;
            imgData.data[i+2] = 0;
        }
    }
    ctx.putImageData(imgData,0,0);
}

function testFunction1() {
    var canvas = document.getElementById(&quot;canvas&quot;);
    var ctx = canvas.getContext(&quot;2d&quot;);
    var imgData = ctx.createImageData(100, 100);
    for (let i = 0; i &lt; imgData.data.length; i+=4) {

        imgData.data[i + 3] = 255;  // alpha: fully visible

        imgData.data[i] = 255;  // red
    }
    ctx.putImageData(imgData,0,0);
}

// it skips some pixels and they are still transparent and they show background color
function testFunction2() {
    var canvas = document.getElementById(&quot;canvas&quot;);
    var ctx = canvas.getContext(&quot;2d&quot;);
    var imgData = ctx.createImageData(100, 100);
    for (let i = 0; i &lt; imgData.data.length; i+=8) {  // &lt;--- i += 8

        imgData.data[i + 3] = 255;  // alpha: fully visible

        imgData.data[i] = 255;  // red
    }
    ctx.putImageData(imgData,0,0);
}

function testFunction3() {
    var canvas = document.getElementById(&quot;canvas&quot;);
    var ctx = canvas.getContext(&quot;2d&quot;);
    var imgData = ctx.createImageData(100, 100);
    for (let i = 0; i &lt; imgData.data.length; i+=4) {

        imgData.data[i + 3] = 255;  // alpha: fully visible

        if(i%8 == 0) {
            imgData.data[i] = 255;  // red
        } else {
            imgData.data[i+1] = 255;  // green
        }
    }
    ctx.putImageData(imgData,0,0);
}
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
